{"database":{"advisory-count":1395,"last-commit":null,"last-updated":null},"lockfile":{"dependency-count":431},"settings":{"target_arch":[],"target_os":[],"severity":null,"ignore":[],"informational_warnings":["unmaintained","unsound","notice"]},"vulnerabilities":{"found":false,"count":0,"list":[]},"warnings":{"unmaintained":[{"kind":"unmaintained","package":{"name":"proc-macro-error","version":"1.0.4","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index","checksum":"da25490ff9892aab3fcf7c36f08cfb902dd3e71ca0f9f9517bea02a73a5ce38c","dependencies":[{"name":"proc-macro-error-attr","version":"1.0.4","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"},{"name":"proc-macro2","version":"1.0.107","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"},{"name":"quote","version":"1.0.47","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"},{"name":"syn","version":"1.0.109","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"},{"name":"version_check","version":"0.9.5","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"}],"replace":null},"advisory":{"id":"RUSTSEC-2024-0370","package":"proc-macro-error","title":"proc-macro-error is unmaintained","description":"proc-macro-error's maintainer seems to be unreachable, with no commits for 2 years, no releases pushed for 4 years, and no activity on the GitLab repo or response to email.\n\nproc-macro-error also depends on `syn 1.x`, which may be bringing duplicate dependencies into dependant build trees.\n\n## Possible Alternative(s)\n\n- [manyhow](https://crates.io/crates/manyhow)\n- [proc-macro2-diagnostics](https://github.com/SergioBenitez/proc-macro2-diagnostics)","date":"2024-09-01","aliases":[],"related":[],"collection":"crates","categories":[],"keywords":[],"cvss":null,"informational":"unmaintained","references":[],"source":null,"url":"https://gitlab.com/CreepySkeleton/proc-macro-error/-/issues/20","withdrawn":null,"license":"CC0-1.0","expect-deleted":false},"affected":null,"versions":{"patched":[],"unaffected":[]}}],"unsound":[{"kind":"unsound","package":{"name":"tokio","version":"1.53.2","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index","checksum":"e95f91fcc7a621e8b030f6aa23c71fe9838ae2fb4d8118b75602a328f5144044","dependencies":[{"name":"bytes","version":"1.12.1","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"},{"name":"libc","version":"0.2.190","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"},{"name":"mio","version":"1.2.4","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"},{"name":"pin-project-lite","version":"0.2.17","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"},{"name":"socket2","version":"0.6.5","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"},{"name":"windows-sys","version":"0.61.2","source":"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"}],"replace":null},"advisory":{"id":"RUSTSEC-2026-0386","package":"tokio","title":"`AsyncFd::new` and related constructors violate I/O safety","description":"`tokio::io::unix::AsyncFd` provides safe constructors (`new`, `with_interest`, `try_new`, and `try_with_interest`) that accept any `T: AsRawFd` rather than requiring proof of ownership or validity of the file descriptor. Because `RawFd` implements `AsRawFd`, safe code can construct an `AsyncFd` from an arbitrary or closed file descriptor, or close/replace the underlying file descriptor while the `AsyncFd` is still alive.\n\nThis violates I/O safety in two ways:\n\n1. `AsyncFd<T>` implements `AsFd` for all `T: AsRawFd` by calling `BorrowedFd::borrow_raw(self.as_raw_fd())`, allowing safe code to obtain an arbitrary `BorrowedFd<'_>` without owning the file descriptor.\n2. Tokio's I/O driver stores a pointer to an internal allocation in the `epoll`/`kqueue` user data for registered file descriptors. If the underlying file descriptor is duplicated and then closed while an `AsyncFd` is alive, and the OS reuses that file descriptor number for a another IO resource on the same runtime, dropping the first `AsyncFd` deregisters the reused file descriptor number and frees the first while the original file description remains registered in `epoll`, leading to a use-after-free when a readiness event fires.\n\nStarting in Tokio 1.51.6, 1.53.3, and 1.54.0, `AsyncFd::new`, `AsyncFd::with_interest`, `AsyncFd::try_new`, and `AsyncFd::try_with_interest` are deprecated in favor of the `unsafe` constructors `AsyncFd::register` and `AsyncFd::register_with_interest`. Callers must ensure that the file descriptor remains valid, open, and unchanged for the lifetime of the `AsyncFd`.","date":"2026-10-13","aliases":[],"related":[],"collection":"crates","categories":["memory-corruption"],"keywords":["io-safety","use-after-free"],"cvss":null,"informational":"unsound","references":["https://github.com/tokio-rs/tokio/issues/8538","https://github.com/tokio-rs/tokio/issues/8539"],"source":null,"url":"https://github.com/tokio-rs/tokio/pull/8587","withdrawn":null,"license":"CC0-1.0","expect-deleted":false},"affected":{"arch":[],"os":[],"functions":{"tokio::io::unix::AsyncFd::new":[">=0.3.2"],"tokio::io::unix::AsyncFd::try_new":[">=1.37.0"],"tokio::io::unix::AsyncFd::try_with_interest":[">=1.37.0"],"tokio::io::unix::AsyncFd::with_interest":[">=0.3.2"]}},"versions":{"patched":[">=1.51.6, <1.52.0",">=1.53.3"],"unaffected":["<0.3.2"]}}]}}
